      "text": "To determine the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This involves solving the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nFirst, rearrange the equation to standard quadratic form:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, let \\( y = x \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 3y + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\(-2\\) to be in the range of \\( f(x) \\), this quadratic equation must have real solutions. Therefore, the discriminant of the quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of \\( y^2 + 3y + c + 2 = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4(1)(c + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
